---
import { DESIGN_IDS } from '../../lib/chart';
import { allSeries, primary, runs, scalingSeries, seriesVersionLabel, usingFixture, DATA_URL } from '../../lib/bench';
// What data the dashboard is showing, plus a stat strip and the cross-engine checksum result.
const s = primary;
const dates = s ? s.runs.map((r) => r.date.slice(0, 10)).sort() : [];
const scaling = scalingSeries();
const hosts = s ? [...new Set(s.runs.map((r) => r.host).filter(Boolean))] : [];

// Checksum agreement: for every configuration where two or more engines ran, do the per-policy checksums match?
let agree = 0;
let total = 0;
if (s) {
	for (const c of s.configs) {
		const rs = DESIGN_IDS.map((d) => s.get(d, c.scale, c.shape)).filter((r) => !!r);
		if (rs.length < 2 || rs.some((r) => !r?.checksums)) continue;
		const policies = Object.keys(rs[0]!.checksums!);
		total++;
		if (policies.every((p) => rs.every((r) => r!.checksums![p] === rs[0]!.checksums![p]))) agree++;
	}
}
---

{
	usingFixture && (
		<div class="viz-note">
			<p>
				<strong>This page shows the development fixture.</strong> No run files exist yet in{' '}
				<code>pg_permissions/bench/baselines/data/</code>, so the charts use numbers that the page parses from the saved
				reports in <code>bench/baselines/</code> (the same numbers as COMPARISON.md). Real data replaces the
				fixture automatically at the next <code>npm run sync</code>.
			</p>
		</div>
	)
}
{
	s ? (
		<>
			<div class="viz-stats">
				<div class="viz-stat">
					<div class="label">Dataset</div>
					<div class="value">{s.label}</div>
					<div class="note">{seriesVersionLabel(s)}</div>
				</div>
				<div class="viz-stat">
					<div class="label">Runs</div>
					<div class="value">{s.runs.length}</div>
					<div class="note">
						{s.configs.length} configurations, {runs.length} runs in all series
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="viz-stat">
					<div class="label">Latest run</div>
					<div class="value">{dates[dates.length - 1]}</div>
					<div class="note">first {dates[0]}</div>
				</div>
				<div class="viz-stat">
					<div class="label">Checksums agree</div>
					<div class="value">
						{agree} of {total}
					</div>
					<div class="note">configurations with identical visible ids on A, B and C</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p>
				The charts use <strong>{s.label}</strong>
				{hosts.length > 0 && <>{' '}on {hosts.join('; ')}</>}: Postgres {s.versions.join(', ')}, the latest run for each design, scale
				and shape. This is the primary series, and we chose it on purpose. It is Docker, the newest Postgres version, and the
				optimized designs when their runs exist (otherwise the newest Docker series, then local).{' '}
				{s.runs[0]?.clients && <>{s.runs[0].clients} pgbench clients, {s.runs[0].duration_s} s for each workload. </>}
				Docker runs cover only the large default, medium deep and medium wide configurations.
				{scaling && scaling !== s && (
					<> The small and medium default scales exist only for <strong>{scaling.label}</strong>, so the scale chart uses that series and says so.</>
				)}{' '}
				A figure never mixes environments or tags. The data has these series: {allSeries.map((x) => x.label).join('; ')}.
				Compare Postgres versions on the <a href={'../postgres-versions/'}>Postgres versions page</a>. Compare the runs before and
				after the optimization pass on the <a href={'../optimizations/'}>Optimizations page</a>. All latencies are in
				milliseconds. There is one run for each configuration, so differences under about 20% are noise. The run files are in{' '}
				<a href={DATA_URL}>bench/baselines/data</a>.
			</p>
		</>
	) : (
		<div class="viz-note">
			<p>The page found no benchmark data (no real runs and no fixture).</p>
		</div>
	)
}
